# K.I.N.G. — example environment file
# ===================================================================
# EVERYTHING HERE IS OPTIONAL in the default (bridge) mode. K.I.N.G. runs
# perfectly with an empty file — the bridge authenticates off your Claude Code
# login, and voice input and output both fall back to the browser's own speech.
# Copy this to .env.local, and add only what you actually want to change.
#
# The one line worth adding is ELEVENLABS_API_KEY, down in the BRIDGE section:
# it gives you a much better voice and Scribe transcription. The free tier is
# plenty for a demo. Never commit a real key.
#
# Two audiences read these variables:
#   - the BRIDGE (the Node server, bridge/server.mjs) reads the JARVIS_* and
#     ELEVENLABS_API_KEY variables. Put them in bridge/.env (copy
#     bridge/.env.example) or in the shell that runs `npm run bridge`; a
#     separate file because this one is Vite's.
#   - the FRONTEND (the Vite app) reads the VITE_* variables from .env.local.
#     Vite's envPrefix is still its default of VITE_, so that is the only
#     prefix which can reach the browser.
#
# The Groq key is the exception that has moved: GROQ_API_KEY below is no longer
# a frontend variable at all. It is read by the proxy in api/chat.ts — and by
# the matching dev middleware in vite.config.ts — so the page never sees it.


# ===================================================================
# BRIDGE (the server) — read from the shell that runs `npm run bridge`
# ===================================================================
# These are shown with their defaults. Leave them unset to accept the default.

# Port the bridge listens on (WebSocket + a few HTTP endpoints). Default 8787.
# If you change it, change VITE_BRIDGE_URL to match.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# The model the bridge asks Claude Code to run. Default claude-opus-5.
# JARVIS_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Reasoning effort. Default medium.
# JARVIS_EFFORT=medium

# Allow effectful tools (send, tap, delete, install, pay). Off by default, so
# JARVIS can look at anything and change nothing. `npm run bridge:writes` sets
# this to 1 for you — read the decideTool gate in bridge/server.mjs first. On
# Windows, where that script's `VAR=1 command` prefix does not work, set
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=1 in bridge/.env instead.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=0

# Extra browser origins allowed to open the agent WebSocket, comma separated.
# Local dev origins are already allowed; add others only if you serve the app
# from a different address. Default: none.
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Allow clients that send no Origin header at all (curl, scripts, native apps).
# Off by default, because that is also what local malware looks like. Default 0.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_NO_ORIGIN=0

# Extra directories the bridge's /file endpoint may serve from, comma separated.
# Your home directory and the system temp directory are always included; this is
# for anything outside them, such as an external drive of footage. Default: none.
# JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS=

# ElevenLabs voice id for the bridge's own speech endpoint. Default: George.
# JARVIS_VOICE_ID=JBFqnCBsd6RMkjVDRZzb

# ElevenLabs API key. Optional. With it, K.I.N.G. speaks in the ElevenLabs voice
# and transcribes with ElevenLabs Scribe; without it, everything still works on
# the browser's own speech. If you already have an elevenlabs MCP server in
# ~/.claude.json, the bridge reads the key from there and you can leave this
# blank. Default: none.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# ===================================================================
# PRODUCTION DATABASE (Supabase / Postgres) - optional
# ===================================================================
# Adding any one of these gives the bridge an `mcp` Postgres server, so K.I.N.G.
# can read live row counts, auth users, logs and storage usage as part of an
# infrastructure check. Set none of them and no database tool is advertised at
# all - a tool that can only fail is worse than no tool.
#
# BRIDGE ONLY. Never give these a VITE_ prefix: that is the one prefix Vite
# copies into the browser bundle, and a connection string in a static file is
# the whole database gone. (GROQ_ is no longer a copying prefix either — it was
# only ever used while the key was read through import.meta.env.)
#
# Supabase hands out two pooler endpoints and they behave differently:
#   session     port 5432, prepared statements, right for interactive queries
#   transaction port 6543, no prepared statements, right for serverless
# Set the one that matches how you connect. First match wins:
# SUPABASE_SESSION_DB_URL=postgres://user:pass@db.example.supabase.co:5432/postgres
# SUPABASE_TRANSACTION_DB_URL=postgres://user:pass@db.example.supabase.co:6543/postgres
# The generic name most tooling uses, and the usual fallback when you only
# have one URI to give.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host:5432/postgres

# ===================================================================
# EXECUTION WORKSPACE & DEPLOYMENT - optional, writes only
# ===================================================================
# king_clone and king_deploy only exist when JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=1. With reads
# only, none of the variables below is read at all.

# Where `king_clone` puts a repository. Default ~/KING-WORKSPACE. Clone into the
# workspace, never into the project directory.
# KING_WORKSPACE=

# Vercel token, for `king_deploy` target "vercel". The deploy reports the
# deployment id it got back, or refuses with no token configured.
# VERCEL_TOKEN=

# GitHub token, used only by `king_deploy` target "github" (workflow dispatch).
# Read tools go through the machine's own `gh` login instead, so this can stay
# empty if you have run `gh auth login`.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# aaPanel, for `king_deploy` target "nginx" (cache purge / config reload).
# Both are required together; with neither set the target refuses.
# AAPANEL_URL=
# AAPANEL_KEY=


# ===================================================================
# FRONTEND (Vite) — put these in .env.local
# ===================================================================

# Which brain the browser talks to. Default: bridge — the mode that works on a
# fresh clone with an empty .env.local and no key anywhere. Set it explicitly
# on Vercel (VITE_BACKEND=groq) to deploy standalone.
#   bridge — HYBRID (default). Talk to the local bridge (recommended for full
#            capability). No API key, every MCP server in your Claude Code
#            config is available, and clone/deploy/database are real.
#   groq   — STANDALONE. The page posts to /api/chat, the proxy in api/chat.ts
#            holds the Groq key, and Three.js core, speech and inference all run
#            in the browser. Deploy it to Vercel with no backend to keep alive.
#            Clone, deploy and the database report "Bridge Offline /
#            Standalone Mode" rather than failing.
#   direct — the browser calls the Claude API itself. Needs VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.
# VITE_BACKEND=bridge

# ===================================================================
# GROQ (the standalone engine) — VITE_BACKEND=groq
# ===================================================================
# Groq Cloud is the cloud brain for standalone mode. The key is SERVER SIDE:
# it is read by the /api/chat proxy (api/chat.ts when deployed,
# vite.config.ts's middleware when developing) and never by import.meta.env,
# so it does not enter the bundle. On Vercel, set it as a plain project
# variable named GROQ_API_KEY — no VITE_ prefix, because a VITE_ prefix is
# what would put it in the page.
#
# Locally the same name works here, because vite.config.ts loads this file
# with loadEnv() rather than with Vite's envPrefix. There is deliberately no
# VITE_GROQ_API_KEY any more: two names for one secret is how one of them ends
# up in a build.
# GROQ_API_KEY=

# The model the proxy asks for, and the one it falls back to when Groq reports
# the primary as unavailable — Groq retires ids without warning (qwen-2.5-32b,
# the original fallback, is decommissioned), so the default below is one
# confirmed present in GET /openai/v1/models. The page shows the fallback on
# the tool badge when it is used.
#
# Both names work. VITE_GROQ_MODEL is read by the page too, for the rail
# label; GROQ_MODEL is the one the proxy answers with in x-king-model.
# GROQ_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.8-27b
# GROQ_MODEL_FALLBACK=openai/gpt-oss-20b
# VITE_GROQ_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.8-27b
# VITE_GROQ_MODEL_FALLBACK=openai/gpt-oss-20b

# Where the bridge listens. Keep the port in step with JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT above.
# Default ws://localhost:8787.
# VITE_BRIDGE_URL=ws://localhost:8787

# Browser text-to-speech engine, used when no ElevenLabs voice is active.
# Default system.
#   system — the browser's built-in speechSynthesis. Instant, on-device.
#   kokoro — a neural voice running in the browser. Better sound, slower to start.
# VITE_TTS_ENGINE=system

# Which Kokoro voice, when VITE_TTS_ENGINE=kokoro. Default bm_george.
# VITE_KOKORO_VOICE=bm_george

# Prefer the ElevenLabs voice from the browser when a key is available.
# Default false.
# VITE_USE_ELEVENLABS=false

# Anthropic API key — DIRECT MODE ONLY. Required if and only if
# VITE_BACKEND=direct, and it ships to the browser, so use it only for a local
# demo. The default bridge mode never reads it. Default: none.
# VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
